## 🗄️ MongoDB Schema Design (Member 2 Implements — All Members Must Know)

> MongoDB stores data as **documents** (JSON-like objects) inside **collections** (equivalent to SQL tables). We have 4 collections.

---

### Collection 1: `users`
Stores both **patients** and **healthcare providers** (differentiated by the `role` field).

| Field | Type | Required | Notes |
|-------|------|----------|-------|
| `_id` | ObjectId | Auto | MongoDB auto-generated unique ID |
| `name` | String | ✅ | Max 100 chars |
| `email` | String | ✅ | Unique, lowercase, validated format |
| `password` | String | ✅ | Bcrypt hashed, **never returned in API responses** |
| `role` | String | ✅ | Enum: `"patient"` or `"provider"` |
| `dateOfBirth` | Date | ❌ | Patient only |
| `gender` | String | ❌ | Enum: `"male"`, `"female"`, `"other"` |
| `phone` | String | ❌ | Max 20 chars |
| `address` | String | ❌ | Max 200 chars |
| `allergies` | String[ ] | ❌ | e.g. `["Penicillin", "Pollen"]` |
| `currentMedications` | String[ ] | ❌ | e.g. `["Metformin 500mg"]` |
| `bloodType` | String | ❌ | e.g. `"O+"` |
| `emergencyContact` | Object | ❌ | `{ name, phone, relationship }` |
| `specialization` | String | ❌ | Provider only (e.g. `"Cardiology"`) |
| `licenseNumber` | String | ❌ | Provider only |
| `consentGiven` | Boolean | ✅ | Must be `true` to register (HIPAA) |
| `consentDate` | Date | ✅ | Timestamp when consent was given |
| `isActive` | Boolean | ✅ | Default `true`; set `false` to deactivate |
| `lastLogin` | Date | ❌ | Updated on every login |
| `createdAt` | Date | Auto | Mongoose timestamps |
| `updatedAt` | Date | Auto | Mongoose timestamps |

**Sample Document:**
```json
{
  "_id": "664a1f...",
  "name": "David Singh",
  "email": "david@example.com",
  "password": "$2b$12$hashedpassword...",
  "role": "patient",
  "dateOfBirth": "1990-05-14",
  "allergies": ["Penicillin"],
  "currentMedications": ["Metformin 500mg"],
  "bloodType": "O+",
  "consentGiven": true,
  "consentDate": "2025-04-15T08:30:00Z",
  "isActive": true,
  "createdAt": "2025-04-15T08:30:00Z"
}
```

**Indexes:**
```
email → unique index  (fast login lookup)
role  → index         (fast provider/patient filtering)
```

---

### Collection 2: `goals`
Tracks daily wellness metrics logged by a patient. One document = one metric entry for one day.

| Field | Type | Required | Notes |
|-------|------|----------|-------|
| `_id` | ObjectId | Auto | |
| `userId` | ObjectId (ref: User) | ✅ | Which patient logged this |
| `type` | String | ✅ | Enum: `"steps"`, `"water"`, `"sleep"`, `"calories"`, `"active_time"` |
| `value` | Number | ✅ | The logged amount (e.g. `3620` steps) |
| `target` | Number | ❌ | The daily goal (e.g. `8000` steps) |
| `unit` | String | ❌ | e.g. `"steps"`, `"ml"`, `"hours"`, `"kcal"`, `"min"` |
| `date` | Date | ✅ | Midnight of that day (e.g. `2025-04-15T00:00:00Z`) |
| `notes` | String | ❌ | Optional note from patient |
| `createdAt` | Date | Auto | |
| `updatedAt` | Date | Auto | |

**Sample Document:**
```json
{
  "_id": "664b2a...",
  "userId": "664a1f...",
  "type": "steps",
  "value": 3620,
  "target": 8000,
  "unit": "steps",
  "date": "2025-04-15T00:00:00Z",
  "notes": "Went for a morning walk"
}
```

**Indexes:**
```
{ userId, date }       → compound index (get all of a user's goals for a specific day)
{ userId, type, date } → compound index (get one type e.g. only "steps" for a user)
```

> [!NOTE]
> Goals are **upserted** (insert or update) by `userId + type + date`. So logging steps twice in a day **updates** the existing entry rather than creating a duplicate.

---

### Collection 3: `reminders`
Stores preventive care appointments and health reminders for patients.

| Field | Type | Required | Notes |
|-------|------|----------|-------|
| `_id` | ObjectId | Auto | |
| `userId` | ObjectId (ref: User) | ✅ | Patient this reminder belongs to |
| `title` | String | ✅ | e.g. `"Annual Blood Test"` |
| `description` | String | ❌ | Extra detail |
| `type` | String | ❌ | Enum: `"checkup"`, `"medication"`, `"vaccination"`, `"lab_test"`, `"custom"` |
| `dueDate` | Date | ✅ | When the reminder is due |
| `status` | String | ✅ | Enum: `"upcoming"`, `"met"`, `"missed"` — Default: `"upcoming"` |
| `createdBy` | ObjectId (ref: User) | ❌ | Could be patient or provider |
| `createdAt` | Date | Auto | |
| `updatedAt` | Date | Auto | |

**Sample Document:**
```json
{
  "_id": "664c3b...",
  "userId": "664a1f...",
  "title": "Annual Blood Test",
  "type": "lab_test",
  "dueDate": "2025-04-22T09:00:00Z",
  "status": "upcoming",
  "createdBy": "664a1f..."
}
```

**Status Logic (computed at read time):**
```
if status === "upcoming" AND dueDate < now  →  display as "missed"
if status === "met"                         →  display as "met" (green)
if status === "upcoming" AND dueDate > now  →  display as "upcoming" (blue)
```

**Indexes:**
```
{ userId, dueDate } → compound index (get reminders sorted by date for a user)
```

---

### Collection 4: `auditlogs` *(HIPAA Compliance)*
Automatically records every sensitive write action (who did what, when, from where).

| Field | Type | Required | Notes |
|-------|------|----------|-------|
| `_id` | ObjectId | Auto | |
| `userId` | ObjectId (ref: User) | ❌ | `null` for unauthenticated actions |
| `action` | String | ✅ | e.g. `"POST /api/patients/me"` |
| `resource` | String | ❌ | The endpoint path |
| `ip` | String | ❌ | Client IP address |
| `userAgent` | String | ❌ | Browser/client info |
| `statusCode` | Number | ❌ | HTTP response code (200, 401, etc.) |
| `createdAt` | Date | Auto | |

**Sample Document:**
```json
{
  "_id": "664d4c...",
  "userId": "664a1f...",
  "action": "PUT /api/patients/me",
  "resource": "/api/patients/me",
  "ip": "192.168.1.5",
  "userAgent": "Mozilla/5.0...",
  "statusCode": 200,
  "createdAt": "2025-04-15T10:45:00Z"
}
```

**Indexes:**
```
{ userId, createdAt } → index (see all actions by a user in time order)
```

---

### Relationships Between Collections

```
users (1) ──────── (many) goals
  │                         ↑ userId references users._id
  │
users (1) ──────── (many) reminders
  │                         ↑ userId references users._id
  │
users (1) ──────── (many) auditlogs
                            ↑ userId references users._id
```

> [!NOTE]
> MongoDB does **not enforce** foreign key relationships automatically (unlike SQL). Mongoose handles this via `ref` and `.populate()` calls in the code.

